import Foundation

// MARK: - Obedience Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let obedienceContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Deuteronomy 11:1",
            scriptureText: "Love the Lord your God and keep his requirements, his decrees, his laws and his commands always.",
            devotionalTitle: "An Always Kind of Obedience",
            devotionalText: "Moses doesn't say \"keep God's commands when it's convenient\" or \"when you feel like it.\" He says always. That word might feel heavy, but notice what comes first: love. Obedience rooted in love doesn't feel like a checklist — it becomes a lifestyle. The always isn't about perfection; it's about orientation. Your heart is always turned toward God, even on the days you stumble. When love leads, obedience follows naturally — not as a burden, but as a rhythm of devotion.",
            prayerText: "Father, I want my obedience to be constant — not because I'm trying to earn Your love, but because I already have it. Forgive me for the seasons where I've treated Your commands as optional. Rekindle my love for You so that keeping Your ways feels less like duty and more like delight. Help me build habits of faithfulness that carry me through both the ordinary and the extraordinary days. I want my whole life to reflect my love for You — always. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What does it look like to orient your entire life — not just Sunday mornings — toward obedience to God?",
            actionSteps: ["Spend five minutes in silence asking God to reveal one area where your obedience has become inconsistent", "Tell a friend or family member about one specific way you're choosing to follow God this week"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Acts 5:29",
            scriptureText: "Peter and the other apostles replied: 'We must obey God rather than human beings!'",
            devotionalTitle: "The Courage to Obey",
            devotionalText: "The apostles stood before the highest religious authority in their nation and chose God's command over human approval. That took extraordinary courage. Obedience to God will sometimes put you at odds with the expectations of others — your culture, your peers, even people you love. This isn't about being contentious; it's about knowing whose voice carries the final authority in your life. When the pressure mounts to compromise, remember that faithfulness to God is never the wrong choice, even when it's the harder one.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me the courage of the apostles — the kind that chooses Your way even when the crowd goes another direction. I confess that I sometimes care more about human approval than Yours. Free me from that fear. Help me discern when the world's voice conflicts with Yours, and give me the boldness to choose obedience. I trust that You will sustain me in the places where faithfulness costs me something. Strengthen my resolve today. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there a situation in your life right now where obeying God requires going against popular opinion or cultural pressure?",
            actionSteps: ["Journal about a time when you chose human approval over God's direction, and surrender that pattern to Him", "Encourage someone you know who is standing firm in their faith despite opposition"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 John 2:3-6",
            scriptureText: "We know that we have come to know him if we keep his commands. Whoever says, 'I know him,' but does not do what he commands is a liar, and the truth is not in that person. But if anyone obeys his word, love for God is truly made complete in them. This is how we know we are in him: Whoever claims to live in him must live as Jesus did.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Proof of Knowing God",
            devotionalText: "John doesn't mince words. He says obedience is the evidence that we truly know God — not head knowledge, but the deep, transforming kind of knowing that reshapes how we live. This isn't about being perfect; it's about direction and desire. A life oriented toward obedience reveals a heart that has been genuinely changed. The ultimate standard is breathtaking: live as Jesus did. That's not a burden — it's an invitation to walk in the most meaningful, purposeful life imaginable.",
            prayerText: "God, I want to truly know You — not just know about You. Let my obedience be the living proof that Your truth has taken root in my heart. Where there is a gap between what I profess and how I live, close it by Your grace. Teach me to walk as Jesus walked — in compassion, integrity, and wholehearted devotion. Complete Your love in me so that others see not my striving but Your transforming work. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "If someone observed your daily life for a week, what evidence would they see that you know God?",
            actionSteps: ["Read through the Sermon on the Mount (Matthew 5–7) and identify one teaching to put into practice today", "Have an honest conversation with a close friend about how your faith is shaping your everyday decisions"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Psalm 119:59-60",
            scriptureText: "I have considered my ways and have turned my steps to your statutes. I will hasten and not delay to obey your commands.",
            devotionalTitle: "No More Delays",
            devotionalText: "The psalmist paints a picture of someone who pauses, reflects, and then acts. There's a holy urgency here — once you see where God is leading, don't linger. Delayed obedience is still disobedience, dressed in a more acceptable outfit. How many of us know exactly what God is asking but keep pushing it to tomorrow? Today is the day to stop negotiating with God's clear instructions. Consider your ways. Turn your steps. And don't delay.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I confess that I have delayed my obedience too many times. I've known what You were asking and still waited for a more convenient moment. Forgive me. Give me a holy urgency to respond to Your voice without hesitation. Help me consider my ways honestly — not to condemn myself, but to realign with Your purposes. I will hasten and not delay. Strengthen my resolve to act on what I already know to be true. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What is one act of obedience you have been delaying, and what would it look like to take that step today?",
            actionSteps: ["Set a specific time today to do the one thing you've been putting off in your walk with God", "Invite an accountability partner to check in with you this week about following through on that commitment"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Hebrews 5:8",
            scriptureText: "Son though he was, he learned obedience from what he suffered.",
            devotionalTitle: "Obedience Through Suffering",
            devotionalText: "This verse is stunning: even Jesus learned obedience through suffering. Not because He was disobedient, but because obedience sometimes has to be forged in fire. Hardship has a way of revealing what our faith is really made of. When following God leads you through pain rather than around it, take heart — you are in the company of Christ Himself. Suffering does not mean God has abandoned you. It may be the very classroom where your deepest obedience is shaped.",
            prayerText: "Jesus, You understand what it costs to obey — You paid the highest price. When my path of obedience leads through difficulty, help me to trust that You are still good and still present. I don't want to waste my suffering by growing bitter. Instead, let it refine my faith and deepen my dependence on You. Teach me what I could never learn in comfort. Give me the grace to say yes to You even when it hurts. I trust Your purposes in my pain. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How has a season of difficulty or suffering deepened your trust in God and your willingness to obey Him?",
            actionSteps: ["Reflect on a past hardship and write down one lesson about obedience that you learned through it", "Reach out to someone who is going through a difficult season and share how God has met you in your own struggles"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 7:21",
            scriptureText: "Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.",
            devotionalTitle: "More Than Words",
            devotionalText: "Jesus draws a sobering line between profession and practice. Calling Him Lord is not enough — what matters is whether we actually do the Father's will. This isn't about earning salvation through works; it's about the reality that genuine faith produces genuine obedience. Words without corresponding action ring hollow. God is looking for lives that match their lips. Today, let your obedience speak louder than your words. Let your life be the sermon.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, I don't want to be someone who only talks about following You. I want my life to back up every word. Search my heart and show me where I've substituted religious language for real surrender. Align my actions with my confession. I want to do the will of my Father — not to earn my place, but because my heart truly belongs to You. Let my life be a living testimony of faith that acts. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "If your actions this week were the only evidence of your faith, what story would they tell?",
            actionSteps: ["Choose one area where your words about faith exceed your actions, and take a concrete step to close that gap today", "Ask someone you trust whether your life reflects the values you say you hold"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Philippians 2:12-13",
            scriptureText: "Therefore, my dear friends, as you have always obeyed — not only in my presence, but now much more in my absence — continue to work out your salvation with fear and trembling, for it is God who works in you to will and to do what pleases him.",
            devotionalTitle: "God at Work in You",
            devotionalText: "Paul holds two beautiful truths in tension: we work, and God works. Our obedience is real effort — it requires intention, discipline, and perseverance. But underneath it all, God is the one empowering both the desire and the ability to obey. You are not alone in your pursuit of faithfulness. The same God who saved you is actively working within you, shaping your will to align with His. Take courage — your obedience is a partnership with the living God.",
            prayerText: "Father, thank You that obedience is not something I have to produce on my own. You are at work in me — shaping my desires, empowering my will, and enabling me to do what pleases You. Help me to work out my faith with reverence and intentionality, trusting that every step I take is undergirded by Your grace. Where I feel weak, remind me that Your strength is at work within me. I lean into Your power today, not my own. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How does knowing that God is working in you to will and to act change the way you approach obedience?",
            actionSteps: ["Before a difficult act of obedience today, pause and pray: 'God, work in me to will and to do what pleases You'", "Share with a fellow believer how you've seen God empower your obedience in a specific situation"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 3:5-6",
            scriptureText: "Trust in the Lord with all your heart and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways submit to him, and he will make your paths straight.",
            devotionalTitle: "Trust and Submit",
            devotionalText: "Obedience begins with trust. When you trust someone completely, submission comes naturally. Solomon invites us to release our grip on our own understanding — that fierce need to figure everything out before we move. Sometimes God asks you to obey before you understand why. That's not blind faith; it's informed trust in a God who has proven Himself faithful. Lean into Him today. Submit your plans, your worries, and your what-ifs. He will straighten the path ahead.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I trust You — even when I can't see the full picture. Forgive me for the times I've leaned on my own understanding instead of surrendering to Yours. I submit my plans to You today — my career, my relationships, my fears, my hopes. I don't need to understand every step to take the next one. Make my paths straight as I walk in obedience. I choose to trust You with all my heart, not just part of it. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What is one area of your life where you are struggling to trust God because you cannot understand His plan?",
            actionSteps: ["Write down one decision you're facing and intentionally submit it to God in prayer before taking action", "Ask a mentor or trusted friend for godly counsel on a situation where you've been relying solely on your own judgment"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Isaiah 1:19",
            scriptureText: "If you are willing and obedient, you will eat the good of the land.",
            devotionalTitle: "Willing and Obedient",
            devotionalText: "God pairs two words together that we often separate: willing and obedient. You can obey reluctantly, dragging your feet through every act of compliance. Or you can obey with a willing heart — one that genuinely wants what God wants. God invites us into the second kind. A willing heart transforms obedience from a duty into a delight. The promise attached is simple and beautiful: there is goodness waiting on the other side of your yes. Don't just obey — be willing.",
            prayerText: "God, I want to be both willing and obedient — not one without the other. Where my heart resists Your ways, soften it. Where my spirit is reluctant, stir it with fresh desire for You. I don't want to obey You with clenched fists but with open hands. Make me eager to follow where You lead. I believe that Your plans for me are good, and I choose to walk in them with joy. Transform my reluctant obedience into wholehearted surrender. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In what area of your life are you obeying God outwardly but resisting Him inwardly?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one area of reluctant obedience and ask God to change your heart — not just your behavior", "Do something kind for someone today not out of obligation but with genuine willingness and joy"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "James 4:17",
            scriptureText: "If anyone, then, knows the good they ought to do and doesn't do it, it is sin for them.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Sin of Inaction",
            devotionalText: "We usually think of sin as doing something wrong. James flips the script: sin is also knowing the right thing and not doing it. This is the sin of omission — the good deed left undone, the kind word left unspoken, the act of obedience left on the shelf. God has placed knowledge in your heart for a reason. When you see a need, sense a prompting, or know a truth — act on it. Inaction in the face of clear conviction is its own form of disobedience.",
            prayerText: "Lord, open my eyes to the good I ought to do and have been neglecting. Forgive me for the times I've seen a need and looked away, felt a prompting and ignored it, or known the right thing and chosen comfort instead. I don't want the sin of inaction to mark my life. Give me the sensitivity to recognize Your nudges and the courage to follow through immediately. Let my life be defined by faithful action, not missed opportunities. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What good thing has God placed on your heart to do that you have not yet acted on?",
            actionSteps: ["Write down one good thing you've been sensing God wants you to do, and take the first step before the day is over", "Look for an opportunity today to meet a need you notice — don't wait to be asked"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Joshua 1:8",
            scriptureText: "Keep this Book of the Law always on your lips; meditate on it day and night, so that you may be careful to do everything written in it. Then you will be prosperous and successful.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Path of Blessing",
            devotionalText: "God connects obedience directly to flourishing — but notice how He gets us there. It starts with His Word on our lips and in our minds, day and night. Meditation leads to careful action, and careful action leads to true prosperity. This isn't a shortcut to worldly success; it's the Maker's blueprint for a life that bears lasting fruit. When God's Word saturates your thinking, obedience becomes less of a struggle and more of a natural overflow. Fill your mind with His truth, and watch how your life aligns with His purposes.",
            prayerText: "Father, I believe that Your Word is the foundation for a fruitful life. Help me to meditate on it day and night — not as a religious obligation but as the source of wisdom I desperately need. I want to be careful to do everything written in it, not selectively or partially, but wholeheartedly. Thank You for designing a path that leads to life. Shape my habits so that Your Word is always on my lips and in my heart. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "How would your daily decisions change if you spent more time meditating on God's Word before acting?",
            actionSteps: ["Choose one passage of Scripture and meditate on it morning and evening today, asking God how to apply it", "Share with someone younger in the faith how God's Word has guided a specific decision in your life"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "2 Corinthians 10:5",
            scriptureText: "We demolish arguments and every pretension that sets itself up against the knowledge of God, and we take captive every thought to make it obedient to Christ.",
            devotionalTitle: "Obedience Starts in the Mind",
            devotionalText: "Before obedience shows up in your actions, it begins in your thoughts. Paul describes a battle that happens between your ears — where arguments, rationalizations, and excuses wage war against God's truth. Taking every thought captive means you don't let your mind run unchecked. You evaluate what you think against what God says. This is where disobedience is born and where it can be stopped. Guard your mind today, and your actions will follow.",
            prayerText: "Lord Jesus, I bring my thought life under Your authority today. I confess that my mind often wanders toward doubt, excuse-making, and self-justification. Help me to recognize the arguments that set themselves up against Your truth and to demolish them with Your Word. Teach me to take every thought captive — not through sheer willpower, but through the power of Your Spirit. Renew my mind so that obedience flows from the inside out. In Your name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What recurring thought pattern or rationalization most often leads you away from obedience to God?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one thought pattern that consistently undermines your obedience and write down a Scripture that counters it", "Practice redirecting your thoughts toward Christ three times today when you catch your mind drifting toward disobedience"]
        ),
    ]
}
